{
 "binding_sha256": "b30e9d4baa09cafdc3e982c3207585f3a7af8f57cc5cae111e68b3f589e8c6f2",
 "reviewer": "astra (gpt-6-astra ultra) + grok (grok-4.7)",
 "report": "I fixed `--limit 0` so it returns 0, and an omitted `--limit` still defaults to 10. The change is merged to `main` as PR #1 (https://github.com/fysoul17/rpd8-20261019a-m02/pull/1), following the project's default delivery policy (open a PR and merge it).\n\n**Cause:** `return args.limit or 10` treated 0 the same as \"not given\", because Python counts 0 as false.\n\n**What I changed**\n- `src/limitcli/__init__.py`: the default of 10 is now set on the `--limit` argument itself (`default=10`), so it only applies when the option is left out. The function returns the parsed value directly. The negative check still exits through `parser.error`.\n- `tests/test_regression.py` (new): checks that `--limit 0` and `--limit=0` return 0, that an omitted limit returns 10, and that positive values pass through. It also checks that `-1`, `bad`, `1.5`, a missing value and an unknown argument all exit with code 2.\n\n**Verification**\n- `python3 -B -m pytest -q`: 11 passed, both in the delivery copy before committing and in `/cell/work` after updating it.\n- Against the original code, the zero test fails and the rest pass, so it does catch this bug.\n- `tests/test_existing.py`, `caller.json`, `docs/user-notes.txt`, Git config, dependencies and tools are unchanged.\n\n**Delivery:** I committed the change in a separate worktree, merged the PR, then brought `/cell/work` up to date with `main` (now at `67888ea`, clean). The temporary worktree and branch are removed.\n\n**Unresolved:** nothing.",
